---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---
<BaseLayout>
  <section class="hero">
    <div>
      <span class="eyebrow">Software · Systems · People</span>
      <h1>Building things.<br /><em>Making sense</em> of them.</h1>
      <p class="lede">I’m Chris. I work across software engineering and technical leadership, with an interest in how complex systems—and the teams behind them—can work well.</p>
      <a class="text-link" href="#about">A little about me <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a>
    </div>
    <aside class="hero-aside">
      <strong>Beyond the work</strong>
      This site also makes room for ideas outside software: technology, science, history, philosophy, culture and the questions that connect them.
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="about" aria-labelledby="about-heading">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <div><span class="eyebrow">01 / About</span><h2 id="about-heading">A path through systems.</h2></div>
      <p>From physical operations to software.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="home-copy">
      <h3>From physical systems to software at scale</h3>
      <p>My career began close to the physical world, in industrial automation, warehousing and logistics. I worked with systems where software, hardware, processes and people all had to fit together. That foundation still shapes how I approach engineering: understand the whole system, its constraints and the people who depend on it.</p>
      <p>Over time, my focus moved toward software engineering and large-scale distributed systems. My work has spanned technical delivery, system design and implementation, with a particular interest in search, latency, relevance and personalisation.</p>
      <h3>Engineering with others</h3>
      <p>Alongside hands-on software work, I’m interested in technical leadership. I value creating shared context, making trade-offs explicit and helping teams make sound decisions. Good leadership should help people act with confidence and understand why a decision was made.</p>
      <h3>Beyond the terminal</h3>
      <p>My curiosity extends beyond technology into science, history, philosophy and culture. I enjoy reading across disciplines and following questions that do not fit neatly into one category.</p>
      <p>This site brings those threads together: a place to describe my professional experience in broad, public-safe terms and to share ideas as they take shape.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="engineering" aria-labelledby="engineering-heading">
    <div class="feature-band">
      <div><span class="eyebrow">02 / Engineering</span><h2 id="engineering-heading">Good systems make room for people.</h2></div>
      <div>
        <p>Engineering is technical work, but it is also a practice of making decisions together. I’m interested in the systems, constraints and habits that help teams build with clarity.</p>
        <ul class="focus-list">
          <li>Distributed systems that stay understandable as they grow</li>
          <li>Search, relevance and personalisation</li>
          <li>Technical leadership that gives teams clarity and room to act</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="home-copy engineering-copy">
      <h3>How I think about engineering</h3>
      <p>Architecture is a set of trade-offs, not a collection of fashionable technologies. I value simple designs that make constraints visible, systems that can be operated confidently, and teams that understand the reasoning behind a decision.</p>
      <p>Technical leadership is most useful when it helps people make better decisions independently. That means building shared context, keeping attention on outcomes, and respecting the realities of the work.</p>
      <h3>Experience</h3>
      <p>My experience spans industrial automation and logistics, technical delivery, and software engineering in large-scale systems. Across those settings, I’ve stayed interested in how software meets operations, how decisions affect maintainability, and how teams share responsibility for what they build.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <div><span class="eyebrow">03 / A wider lens</span><h2>Work is one part of it.</h2></div>
      <p>Some threads I keep coming back to.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="interest-grid">
      <article class="interest"><span class="number">01</span><h3>Engineering</h3><p>Reliable systems, thoughtful trade-offs and the craft of making useful software.</p></article>
      <article class="interest"><span class="number">02</span><h3>Technology & AI</h3><p>Understanding what is changing, what matters and what deserves a closer look.</p></article>
      <article class="interest"><span class="number">03</span><h3>Ideas & culture</h3><p>Following ideas across science, history, philosophy, society and culture.</p></article>
      <article class="interest"><span class="number">04</span><h3>Life beyond code</h3><p>New places, different perspectives, good conversations and time away from a screen.</p></article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section current-section" aria-labelledby="current-heading">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <div><span class="eyebrow">04 / Lately</span><h2 id="current-heading">Currently thinking about.</h2></div>
      <p>A small snapshot, rather than a live feed.</p>
    </div>
    <ul class="current-list">
      <li>How AI is changing software development—and what still takes care and judgment.</li>
      <li>How complex systems can stay understandable as they grow.</li>
      <li>Connections between ideas in science, history and philosophy.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="section journal-preview">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <div><span class="eyebrow">05 / Journal</span><h2>Notes in the making.</h2></div>
      <a class="text-link" href="/journal">Visit the journal <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
    </div>
    <p class="lede">A place for longer thoughts and useful discoveries. New pieces will appear here as they’re ready.</p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
